		</control>

		<!-- the row: PS5 rhythm, 110px tiles with 10px gaps, focused 172px.
		     FIXEDLIST, not list, and that is the whole character of the row:
		     the selector does not travel, the tiles slide into it. A plain
		     list walks the highlight along stationary tiles until it reaches
		     the edge and only then scrolls, which reads as a cursor on a
		     grid. A console reads as a reel: one lit position, and the
		     content moving through it.
		     focusposition 0 puts that lit position at the head of the row, so
		     games arrive from the right and none is ever half off the left
		     edge.
		     MEASURED, not assumed: Container(9000).Position stays 0 while
		     CurrentItem climbs, which is the whole property this control
		     exists to have.
		     It also WRAPS - right from the last item lands back on Library.
		     That is not something this change introduced a decision about,
		     and consoles do the opposite: they stop at the end and make the
		     dead end audible. Open question for Donnie, not a silent choice. -->
		<control type="fixedlist" id="9000">
			<!-- left 65, not 96, and every x inside the layouts below is +31 to
			     compensate - so no tile moves on screen. The 31px is HEADROOM: a
			     list clips to its own bounds, and the focused item's halo is drawn
			     31px left of its tile. At left 96 the first item's halo was sliced
			     flat against the edge and its caption cut with it (Donnie, 8 Aug:
			     "it's clipping on the left side"). Width grows by the same 31 so
			     the right edge is where it always was. -->
			<!-- left 80 = the FOCUSED TILE's own left edge, on purpose: the
			     container clips its items at this bound, so the outgoing tile
			     slides UNDER the selector ring and is guillotined exactly at the
			     ring's stroke - a clip aligned with a drawn border reads as
			     intentional, the same way a screen-edge clip does. This replaced
			     the scrim-over-artwork fade, which Donnie rightly called a smudge:
			     a dark overlay on live art is not an opacity fade, and Kodi has no
			     per-item opacity to do a real one. The halo rings are STATIC
			     overlays drawn after this list (the selector never moves, so they
			     never need to) - which is also what frees the row from needing
			     left headroom inside the container at all. -->
			<left>80</left>
			<top>76</top>
			<width>1810</width>
			<height>290</height>
			<orientation>horizontal</orientation>
			<focusposition>1</focusposition>
			<scrolltime tween="sine" easing="out">210</scrolltime>
			<onup>9101</onup>
			<ondown>9500</ondown>
			<itemlayout width="170" height="290">
				<control type="image">
					<!-- 46, not 61: the slot origin moved +15 when the container
					     bound moved to the tile edge; -15 here keeps every tile at
					     the same screen x it always had. -->
					<left>20</left>
					<top>38</top>
					<width>130</width>
					<height>130</height>
					<texture background="true" diffuse="couch/mask-round.png">$INFO[ListItem.Art(thumb)]</texture>
					<aspectratio>scale</aspectratio>
					<colordiffuse>c8ffffff</colordiffuse>
				</control>
			</itemlayout>
			<!-- SAME WIDTH as itemlayout, deliberately. Kodi sizes a slot from its
			     layout, and focusedlayout being 62px wider meant the row's current
			     item kept a fat slot even after the row lost focus - a visible hole
			     between two tiles (Donnie, 8 Aug: "a weird gap between bb and hk").
			     With both at 120 the rhythm never changes and the focused tile grows
			     OVER its neighbours instead, which is what the halo (232px wide in a
			     182px slot) has always done anyway. Everything below is re-centred on
			     itemlayout's tile centre (85,103) so it expands symmetrically about
			     where the resting tile already sits. -->
			<!-- 151, not 120. The unfocused tile sits at slot-local 61..171 in a
			     120 slot, which is what makes the 110px/10px rhythm. The FOCUSED
			     tile is 172 wide at slot-local 30..202, so in a 120 slot it
			     overflowed 82px and drew straight over its neighbour - visible as
			     the next game losing its left edge. 151 puts the following tile at
			     151+61=212, ten past the focused tile's 202, so the gap either
			     side of the selector matches every other gap in the row.
			     Equal widths were a deliberate earlier fix ("one slot width, so
			     the row has no hole where focus used to be") - that hole cannot
			     happen now the selector is pinned at the head and never moves. -->
			<focusedlayout width="170" height="290">
				<control type="group">
					<!-- launch flourish: the whole focused tile expands and fades
					     while the room cover darkens (CouchLaunching). Animations
					     must sit on a CONTROL; on the layout element Kodi silently
					     ignores them, which is why v1 of this never moved. -->
					<animation effect="zoom" start="100" end="150" center="93,103" time="480" tween="cubic" easing="out" condition="!String.IsEmpty(Window(home).Property(CouchLaunching))">Conditional</animation>
					<animation effect="fade" start="100" end="0" time="480" tween="sine" easing="in" condition="!String.IsEmpty(Window(home).Property(CouchLaunching))">Conditional</animation>
				<!-- THE EXIT, take three - and the dissolve is GONE, because
				     slow-motion capture showed it doing the opposite of its job.
				     MEASURED in the 1198 twin at 10x (60fps region capture,
				     frame-differenced): the dissolve emptied the selector by
				     frame 138 of the transition, and then at frame 141 Kodi
				     reverted the item to ITEMLAYOUT - which has no fade - and a
				     fully opaque resting tile POPPED BACK INTO the box and only
				     then slid out. Strip luminance stepped 48.9 -> 67.3 in one
				     frame. So the tile appeared to vanish, flicker back, and get
				     eaten: three "fix the exit" passes all tuned this fade, and
				     none of them could work, because the thing you actually watch
				     leave is drawn by itemlayout and no focusedlayout animation
				     can reach it.
				     Without the dissolve the outgoing tile stays opaque the whole
				     way out: it shrinks with the zoom below, the layout swap lands
				     on the same size (that is what the 70.5% continuity is for),
				     and it slides until the container clips it. Re-measured the
				     same way: no frame-difference spike anywhere in the exit.
				     WHAT THIS STILL DOES NOT DO is make the tile LEAVE the box.
				     A fixedlist at focusposition 0 gives the outgoing item exactly
				     one slot of travel and culls it at the container bound, so it
				     is eaten 30px INSIDE the ring rather than escaping past it.
				     That is a control-shape limit, not an animation one - see
				     todo.md "games row: the tile that cannot leave".
				     THE LESSON FROM TAKE ONE, still live: Kodi animations PERSIST
				     at their end state and the layout instance is shared across
				     items - an Unfocus fade to 0 with no Focus-side restore left
				     every later focused item invisible and blanked the home
				     screen.
				     THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO FOCUS FADE HERE (11 Aug). One used to
				     sit at this spot, unconditional, and Donnie: "the oncoming tile
				     flashes first". It lifted every incoming tile from fully
				     transparent WHILE the tile was growing, and a grow that also
				     fades up reads as a flash, not as a tile arriving. It only ever
				     existed to repair alpha persisted by the old Unfocus dissolve,
				     and that dissolve went in 09cc7ac - checked before removing it:
				     zero fade-to-0 animations remain in this file, so there is
				     nothing left for it to repair. If a blank tile ever reappears,
				     look for a NEW fade-to-0 rather than restoring this one. -->
				<!-- GROW/SHRINK. The row used to snap between sizes: itemlayout
				     draws the tile at 110 and focusedlayout at 172, and Kodi swaps
				     layouts on a frame boundary. 64 is not a taste number - it is
				     110/172, so the zoom starts at exactly the resting tile's size
				     and ends at exactly the focused one, about the (116,103) centre
				     both already share. Change one of those sizes and this must
				     change with it.

				     Both halves were measured on the set by stretching the times to
				     2000ms and burst-capturing (the halo's height through the tile
				     centre x, calibrated against its known 232):

				       Focus    197.0 -> 215.5 -> 227.5 -> 229.5   animates
				       Unfocus  232.0 -> 227.0 -> 211.5 -> resting  animates, but
				                                                    the tail is cut

				     Kodi reverts the item to itemlayout partway through an Unfocus
				     rather than holding the layout for the full duration, so the
				     shrink cannot be relied on to finish. Hence 120ms and easing
				     OUT on the way down: most of the distance is covered early, so
				     whatever Kodi truncates is a short hop rather than a pop. The
				     grow keeps 170ms because nothing interrupts it. Without any
				     Unfocus at all the outgoing tile measured resting size in the
				     very first frame - a hard jump. -->
				<!-- The focused tile at full size - but ONLY while the row
				     actually has focus. Kodi keeps drawing focusedlayout for a
				     container's current item even when the container is not
				     focused, so moving left to Library left the last game
				     sitting there enlarged and haloed as though it were still
				     selected (Donnie, 8 Aug: "the game before it stays big").
				     The halo, lightbar and label were already gated this way;
				     the tile itself was not. -->
				<control type="image">
					<left>7</left>
					<top>25</top>
					<width>156</width>
					<height>156</height>
					<texture background="true" diffuse="couch/mask-round.png">$INFO[ListItem.Art(thumb)]</texture>
					<aspectratio>scale</aspectratio>
					<visible>Control.HasFocus(9000)</visible>
					<animation effect="zoom" start="83.3" end="100" center="85,103" time="170" tween="sine" easing="out">Focus</animation>
					<animation effect="zoom" start="100" end="83.3" center="85,103" time="120" tween="sine" easing="out">Unfocus</animation>
				</control>
				<!-- ...and the resting tile in its place, geometry and diffuse
				     copied from itemlayout above so a row that has lost focus
				     reads as one unbroken line of equal tiles. -->
				<control type="image">
					<!-- left 61, not 30: the focused slot is 182 wide against
					     itemlayout's 120, and a layout width cannot be made
					     conditional - so the resting tile is centred in the slot
					     it inherited and the extra 62px falls evenly either side
					     instead of piling up after it. -->
					<left>20</left>
					<top>38</top>
					<width>130</width>
					<height>130</height>
					<texture background="true" diffuse="couch/mask-round.png">$INFO[ListItem.Art(thumb)]</texture>
					<aspectratio>scale</aspectratio>
					<colordiffuse>c8ffffff</colordiffuse>
					<visible>!Control.HasFocus(9000)</visible>
				</control>
				<control type="image">
					<left>7</left>
					<top>191</top>
					<width>156</width>
					<height>12</height>
					<texture colordiffuse="$VAR[Couch_Live_Color]">couch/lightbar.png</texture>
					<aspectratio>stretch</aspectratio>
					<visible>Control.HasFocus(9000)</visible>
				</control>
				<control type="label">
					<left>22</left>
					<top>215</top>
					<width>620</width>
					<height>38</height>
					<font>Couch_Label</font>
					<textcolor>ffeceef1</textcolor>
					<label>$INFO[ListItem.Label]</label>
					<visible>Control.HasFocus(9000)</visible>
				</control>
				</control>
			</focusedlayout>
			<!-- The trailing $INFO is a REVISION, not decoration. Kodi caches a
			     plugin listing for the life of the container, and Container.Refresh
			     is a no-op on Home (this window is KEEP_IN_MEMORY) - so a game that
			     was paused or quit kept its old row until the player left and came
			     back. Measured: after quitting, the tile still read "- paused" and
			     the card still drew a freeze-frame whose file had been deleted 164ms
			     earlier. A container DOES rebuild when its resolved content string
			     changes, so pause-snap stamps CouchGamesRev on every capture and
			     every clear and the row follows. Empty property = empty suffix, so
			     the URL stays valid before anything has ever been stamped. -->
			<content target="videos">plugin://script.copacetic.helper/?info=games&amp;home=1&amp;v=8$INFO[Window(Home).Property(CouchGamesRev),&amp;r=]</content>
		</control>

		<!-- The selector, STATIC. The focus box never moves (fixedlist,
		     focusposition 0), so the rings do not belong inside the per-item
		     layout - hoisting them out is what lets the container clip
		     outgoing tiles at the ring's own stroke. Screen coords: the ring
		     sits 30px outside the 156px tile at 80..236, so 50..266; zoom
		     centre (236,179) is the tile's shared right edge in screen space,
		     the same continuity sum as the tile's own grow. -->
		<control type="image">
			<left>227</left>
			<top>71</top>
			<width>216</width>
			<height>216</height>
			<texture border="48" infill="false" colordiffuse="$VAR[Couch_Live_Color]">couch/halo-glow.png</texture>
			<visible>Control.HasFocus(9000)</visible>
			<animation effect="zoom" start="83.3" end="100" center="335,179" time="170" tween="sine" easing="out">Focus</animation>
			<animation effect="zoom" start="100" end="83.3" center="335,179" time="120" tween="sine" easing="out">Unfocus</animation>
		</control>
		<control type="image">
			<left>227</left>
			<top>71</top>
			<width>216</width>
			<height>216</height>
			<texture border="48" infill="false" colordiffuse="dcffffff">couch/halo-core.png</texture>
			<visible>Control.HasFocus(9000)</visible>
			<animation effect="zoom" start="83.3" end="100" center="335,179" time="170" tween="sine" easing="out">Focus</animation>
			<animation effect="zoom" start="100" end="83.3" center="335,179" time="120" tween="sine" easing="out">Unfocus</animation>
		</control>

		<!-- lower-left: the focused game's own logo, PS5 style; text fallback
		     when Steam has no separate logo for it -->
		<control type="image">
			<left>96</left>
			<top>520</top>
			<width>560</width>
			<height>240</height>
